r 2nH(Y ) possible output sequences. Remark 5: Zontak & Irani (2011) postulate that patches of the same images are internally repeated, but unlikely to be found in other images. This notion might appear as a contradiction to the proposed analysis. However, note that although the probability of finding a sequence that belongs to An\u01eb is close to one, the probability of finding a specific sequence in the typical set is very small.\nThe AEP tells us that there exists a relatively small group of training examples that would be sufficient for generalization. However the AEP property does not define this set, nor the correct coding, learning or prediction method. It just reassures us that there exists a set of the sort. How do we find the typical learning set? One option may be by predefined or learned dictionary coding: build a training set that represents the typical set, consisting of the most common structures, in a similar manner to universal source coding based on a known dictionary (Cover & Thomas, 2006)."
