pted and speckle-suppressed tomograms of chicken tissue of size 341\u00d7 691 used for training. The speckle-suppressed tomogram in fig. 4(b) has a homogeneous appearance, whereas the speckled signal in fig. 4(a) is strongly textured, exhibiting a grainy pattern\nwith spots of bright, high intensity signal, alternating with regions of very low signal. Figures 4(c)(d) show the speckle-corrupted and speckle-suppressed tomograms of the blueberry used for testing. The analysis patch size was [Lt, N ] = [9, 9]. As shown in the inferred result in fig. 4(e), the system is able to significantly reduce speckle while maintaining spatial resolution, despite the structural differences between the training and the testing images.\nFigures 5(a)-(b) show the original and compounded tomograms of chicken skin used for training. A tomogram of size 300 \u00d7 643 was used for training. Visibly the training data is nonstationary and of complex structure. Here, the lateral and axial spatial sampling spacing are \u2206x = 2.5\u00b5m,\u2206z = 4.78\u00b5m, respectively. Figures 5(c)-(d) show the original and compounded tomograms of cucumber used for testing. The cucumber image was acquired using a different sample objective lens resulting in twice poorer lateral resolution. The lateral sampling space was increased from 2.5\u00b5m to 8\u00b5m, resulting in system mismatch between training and testing data, in addition to the tissue mismatch. We set the analysis patch size to [8,7], to match the testing data parameters. As can be seen in fig. 5(e), the learning system is able to detect most features and to significantly remove speckle. Horizontal lines appear slightly sharper than vertical ones.\nComputational resources. The proposed framework training time is only 35.00 seconds on a standard CPU workstation equipped with an Intel(R) Core(TM) i7 -7820HQ CPU @ 2.30 GHz, or alternatively 4.56 seconds on average on a laptop GPU NVIDIA GeForce GTX Ti 1650 with 4GB video memory, and converges typically in less than 700 iterations."
